// Reverse-engineered LLVM IR -> AIR lowering for Apple Silicon GPUs

MLIR is the right abstraction for portable performance across heterogeneous
hardware, but it has no Apple Silicon GPU backend [^1]. Mojo proved that
targeting Apple GPUs via LLVM->AIR->MetalLib works, but their implementation
is closed source [^2].

This is a small Python library that provides an open source implementation of
the missing piece: lowering plain LLVM IR to Apple's Intermediate
Representation (AIR), then packaging and running it through Metal.

  +----------------+      +----------------+      +----------------+
  |    LLVM IR     |----->|   AIR (text)   |----->|    Metallib    |
  | (Open Source)  |      | (Proprietary)  |      |    (Binary)    |
  +----------------+      +----------------+      +----------------+
          [ src/llvm_to_air.py ]   [ src/air_to_metallib.py ]   |
                                                                v
                                                  +----------------+
                                                  |   Apple GPU    |
                                                  |   (M-Series)   |
                                                  +----------------+

Fair warning: this is experimental and brittle. AIR is closed source and
undocumented, so everything here is reverse engineered.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Library
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

src/llvm_to_air.py
    to_air(llvm_ir_text, kernel_overrides=None) -> str
        Lower LLVM IR text to AIR LLVM IR text (target triple, datalayout,
        address spaces, air.* intrinsics and kernel metadata).

src/air_to_metallib.py
    compile_to_metallib(air_llvm_ir) -> bytes
        Assemble AIR into a .metallib via `xcrun metal` / `xcrun metallib`.
    create_compute_pipeline(metallib_binary, kernel_name) -> (device, pso)
        Load the metallib and build a Metal compute pipeline state.
    execute_kernel(device, pso, grid_size, threadgroup_size, encode_args_fn)
        Encode arguments, dispatch, and wait for completion.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example: handwritten LLVM IR matmul
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

See `test/test_llvm_to_air_matmul.py` for a complete, runnable example: it
lowers a handwritten LLVM IR matmul kernel with `to_air`, builds it with
`compile_to_metallib` and `create_compute_pipeline`, runs it with
`execute_kernel`, and checks 2x2 and 3x3 results against known values.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Tests
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

    $ make test

This runs pytest through `uv` with pyobjc's Metal bindings. Most tests
compile kernels and run them on the GPU, so they need macOS on Apple
Silicon with the Apple Metal toolchain installed (Xcode or the Command Line
Tools providing `xcrun metal` and `xcrun metallib`). Without the toolchain,
GPU checks can't run. The translator unit tests run on CPU only:

    $ python3 -m unittest discover -s test -p test_llvm_to_air_unit.py

Design notes on GPUs and on the reverse engineering of AIR live in `docs/`.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
